---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SideMark from '../../components/SideMark.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Stores Designer — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Design-system tooling at Amazon: helping many teams build one consistent shopping surface without each of them coding it by hand."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="back"><a href="/">← Index</a></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-10">
      <SideMark href="/work/stores-designer/" />
      <h1>Stores Designer</h1>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <p class="dek c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      Design-system tooling at Amazon. A lot of teams build one shopping surface, and my
      job is to make sure they don't each build it their own way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- TYPE A with no clip: this is current production work at Amazon, so there is nothing
       public to show. The masthead + claim/evidence beats still run — the clip beat is the
       one part of the type that content can be missing. -->
  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
    <div class="facts">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">Frontend engineer — the tooling, and the design system underneath it.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Where</div>
        <div class="ftx">At Amazon · 2023–present.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="facts" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Simplest form</div>
        <div class="ftx">Give teams a design system and a tool that builds on it, so the consistent way to ship a page is also the fastest way.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Proof</div>
        <div class="ftx">
          Internal, and staying that way. The public version of this problem is
          <a href="/work/luthier/">Luthier</a>, which I built at Amazon three years
          earlier and can show.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          Many teams, one shopping surface. Left alone, each team builds its own version
          of the same component and the surface drifts apart — slightly different
          spacing, slightly different behaviour, a hundred times over.
        </p>
        <p>
          Telling people to be consistent doesn't work; nobody has time to check.
          Building the shared thing so it's the quickest route to a shipped page does.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TYPE A close: named, last. "Where it stands" rather than "Outcome" — this work is
       still running, and an "Outcome" head on unfinished work overclaims. -->
  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          Front-end for the tooling, and the design system it builds against. Most of what
          I've learned about working with agents came out of this: the loops on
          <a href="/method/">the method page</a> are the ones I run here, on a system
          where a wrong component ships to a lot of pages at once.
        </p>
        <p class="fine">
          Production work at Amazon, so this page stays high level. No internal detail.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
